+---
+name: tool-powershell
+description: >-
+  Drive PowerShell (the PowerShell tool, powershell.exe or pwsh.exe from WSL,
+  or a .ps1 script) without burning repeated failures. Use it when a PowerShell
+  command exits 1 with no useful message, when the same command has failed
+  twice, when Remove-Item or another write is refused on a protected system
+  path, when a Windows script is called from bash and the quoting breaks, or
+  before writing a new .ps1 file.
+license: MIT
+compatibility: >-
+  A session that runs PowerShell 5.1 or 7 on Windows, directly or through WSL
+  interop. Not for plain Linux shells.
+---
+
+# PowerShell without repeated failures
+
+Most repeated PowerShell failures are one of four things: an exit code that
+says nothing, a retry of a failure that cannot change, a refusal that is a
+policy and not a bug, or quoting that bash mangled before PowerShell saw it.
+
+## 1. Make an exit 1 say why
+
+A bare `Exit code 1` means a non-terminating error went by or a script ended
+with `exit 1` and no message. Before you run it again:
+
+- Put `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'` at the top of every script you write or
+  edit, so the first error stops the run at its own line.
+- Wrap the body in `try { ... } catch { ... }`. In the catch, write
+  `$_.Exception.Message` and `$_.InvocationInfo.PositionMessage` to the error
+  stream (`[Console]::Error.WriteLine(...)` or `Write-Error`), then
+  `exit 1`.
+- Never hide an error with `-ErrorAction SilentlyContinue` or an empty catch
+  to get a clean exit. A hidden error comes back later as a different failure.
+- Use `-NoProfile` for one-off commands, so a user profile cannot add output
+  or errors of its own.
+
+## 2. Two failures means change something
+
+If the same command has failed twice, a third identical run is wasted. Read the
+message, change the command or the approach, or stop and report. Retry only a
+failure that can pass on its own (a locked file, a network blip) and cap it at
+three tries with a pause. Access denied, path protected and file not found do
+not get better by waiting.
+
+## 3. A protected path is a stop sign
+
+`Remove-Item ... is blocked. This path is protected from removal` (and any
+"Access is denied" on a system location) is a deliberate refusal. Do not
+retry it, add `-Force` or `-Recurse`, switch to `cmd /c rd`, `[IO.Directory]::Delete`
+or `robocopy /MIR` to get around it, or rename the folder first.
+
+- Keep every delete inside a directory the task created, such as a scratch
+  folder under `$env:TEMP`. Resolve the path with `Resolve-Path` and refuse to
+  delete unless the result starts with that folder.
+- Run a destructive command with `-WhatIf` first and read what it lists.
+- For anything outside that folder, write down the exact command and what it
+  removes in a hand-over note for the owner (`HANDOFF.md` in the repo, or the
+  final message) and leave the system untouched. For elevation failures see
+  the `windows-elevation-from-wsl` skill.
+
+## 4. Quoting from bash
+
+`powershell.exe -Command "..."` inside bash is double-quoted, so bash expands
+`$_`, `$env:X` and `$(...)` before PowerShell runs. The symptom is a parse
+error about a missing property or an unexpected token.
+
+- Prefer `-File script.ps1` and put the logic in the script.
+- For a one-liner, use single quotes around the whole `-Command` text; inside
+  it use double quotes for PowerShell strings.
+- Pass values as parameters (`-File script.ps1 -Name "$name"`), not spliced into
+  the command text.
+
+## 5. New .ps1 files
+
+Keep new scripts ASCII only (use `-` for a dash, `->` for an arrow): Windows
+PowerShell 5.1 reads a file without a byte order mark as the ANSI code page
+and a smart quote can end a string early. Start with `[CmdletBinding()]`,
+`param(...)` and `$ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'`, and finish with an
+explicit exit code.
